ServerError::InvalidRequest { reason } fields are passed through to client error responses
Link: https://chatgpt.com/codex/cloud/security/findings/argmin-security-review-2026-06-23-l2
Criticality: low (attack path: low)
Status: invalid

# Resolution
Reviewed and marked invalid.

The factual observation is true: several `ServerError` variants clone their `reason` into the client-facing S3 error `Message`, and malformed `Content-Length` is one request-controlled example. That does not make the finding a security issue. Reflecting malformed request details back to the same caller is normal S3-style error behavior, the values are bounded by HTTP/header/body parser limits, and the response XML escapes markup characters, so this is not XML or body injection.

The report's own example is also not an information disclosure: the client already supplied the `Content-Length` value. The code should continue to avoid putting server-internal details or secrets in client-facing `reason` strings, but this finding does not identify such a reachable path.

One adjacent hardening observation remains: the generic XML escaper escapes markup but does not normalize XML-illegal control characters. A direct unit/property test with arbitrary internal `reason` strings could demonstrate invalid XML, but that would be an artificial internal input rather than the reported reachable attack. If a real request path is found that can reflect XML-illegal characters into an error message, the fix should be central XML serialization hardening rather than treating all dynamic S3 error messages as leaks.

