# Summary
`client_error_message` (`crates/server-http/src/http/response.rs:159-310`) maps `ServerError::InvalidRequest { reason }` and several related variants directly to the `Message` field of the S3 error XML, which is sent to the client. The body is then run through `xml::xml_escape` (`crates/server-http/src/http/xml.rs:2594-2605`), so the response is well-formed XML, but the `reason` string can contain attacker-controlled data (bucket name, key name, upload ID, copy-source header value) before it reaches a validator. Most call sites do validate the inputs first, but a few do not — for example, `ServerError::InvalidURI { reason: "Couldn't parse the specified URI.".to_string() }` is constructed in `percent_decode_strict` (`crates/server-http/src/http/request.rs:368-370`) with a static string, but `ServerError::InvalidRequest { reason: format!("invalid Content-Length: {value}") }` (`crates/server-http/src/http/request.rs:34-39`) interpolates the raw `Content-Length` header value. A `Content-Length: <weird>` value is sent back to the client in the error body. This is not a vulnerability per se (the value is bounded, the response is well-formed XML, the client already sent the value), but it leaks the raw header back to the caller, which can be used to confirm a request reached the parser, and it is a regression vector for future code that builds a `reason` from a less-validated source. A similar concern applies to `ServerError::InvalidRequest { reason: "POST form missing 'key' field" }` and friends — these are static and safe, but the pattern is mixed and a future change could leak more.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm `client_error_message` passes `reason` through to the error XML body.
- [x] Confirm the body is XML-escaped via `xml::xml_escape` (no header/body injection).
- [x] Confirm at least one site interpolates a request-controlled value into `reason` (`Content-Length`).
- [x] Add a property test asserting the rendered XML `Message` field never contains control characters or unescaped `<`/`>`.
## Report
Grep for `ServerError::InvalidRequest { reason: format!` and `ServerError::InvalidArgument { reason: format!` in `crates/server-http/src/http/` and `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/` shows a mix of static strings (safe) and interpolated request values (mostly bounded to validated inputs). The most exposed site is `validate_content_length` at `crates/server-http/src/http/request.rs:34-39`, which echoes the raw `Content-Length` value into the error. The fix is to either (a) normalise the echoed value (e.g. truncate to 64 bytes, strip non-printable characters) before passing it into the error, or (b) replace the dynamic `reason` with a static message ("invalid Content-Length header") and rely on the request_id/host_id for diagnostics. The same audit should be performed on `InvalidURI`, `InvalidRedirectLocation`, `MalformedPolicy`, `MalformedXML`, `MalformedPOSTRequest`, `MalformedChunkedBody`, `MalformedTrailerError`, and `InvalidTag` — all of which currently clone the `reason` into the response.

